+constraints are \emph{at most once} for optional bins and \emph{exactly once}
+for mandatory ones; the pricing subproblem maximizes profit rather than
+minimizing distance, so route extension stops naturally once a detour costs
+more than  the waste it collects; and branching resolves the visit/no-visit
+decision first, through hierarchical node-visitation branching, and only then
+branches on arcs by the divergence rule. Beyond the base formulation,
+lifted cover inequalities~\cite{Gu1999LiftedCI} on saturated arcs are separated,
+following the reference methodology, with their duals entering pricing as
+arc-cost adjustments; rounded-capacity, subset-row, multi-star and
+edge-clique separation is also wired, while the minimum-cut, triangle-clique
+and node-profit engines are inert in the reviewed revision (they are
+constructed but never add a cut). The saved configurations use depth-first
+search, at most $2{,}000$ branch nodes, ng-neighbourhoods of size $12$, a
+$15$\,s pricing timeout, at most $50$ routes per pricing round, and at most
+$150$ column-generation iterations; they also enable a strong-branching
+heuristic subsequently found to produce suboptimal plans
+(Sect.~\ref{sec:integrity}). What the archived executable did with these
+switches is pending recovery of the run records
+(Sect.~\ref{sec:integrity}).
+
+Both exact methods run under a configured per-run budget in these
+experiments ($60$\,s of search; an additional short solve may follow).
+Neither is run to certified optimality: the BPC engine additionally stops
+once its optimality gap falls below $0.5\%$, or $1\%$ under early
+termination. If the search ends with no integer-feasible node, BPC falls
+back to the better of the mandatory-only greedy warm start and the
+integer-restricted restricted master over the columns generated so far;
+otherwise it returns the best integer node. SWC-TCF returns its best
+incumbent. Their results should be read as ``best solution found within
+budget'', not as proven optima.
 
