+where $\phi_{sh}$ is the pheromone on the transition from state $s$ to
+heuristic $h$ (Chen et al.\ write $\tau_{ij}$; we use $\phi$ to avoid the
+clash with the horizon length $\tau$), $\eta_{sh}$ is heuristic information
+about $h$'s likely efficacy, and $\alpha,\beta$ weight the two. A walk is
+evaluated when it completes: if it improved the solution, a deposit
+$\Delta\phi = Q_{\mathrm{dep}} + I_k / L_k$ is laid along the transitions it
+used, so operator \emph{sequences} that work well together are reinforced
+rather than merely individually good operators; the first transition of a
+journey was deposited on a virtual row that selection never read, so the
+opening hop of an improving walk went unreinforced in the reviewed
+implementation (corrected since). Pheromone evaporates at rate
+$r_{\mathrm{evap}}$ per iteration, $\phi \leftarrow (1 - r_{\mathrm{evap}})\,
+\phi$ before depositing; with the archived $r_{\mathrm{evap}} = 0.5$ this
+coincides numerically with the retention convention of Chen et al.\
+($\phi \leftarrow 0.5\,\phi$). A faster-adapting visibility signal modulates
+selection on a shorter timescale: each operator's improvement contribution,
+$\lambda^{I}$ in the paper-faithful form or a running-mean-normalised
+exponential in the shipped default, is accumulated per transition and
+normalised by that transition's usage count. The implementation under which
+the archived runs executed divided this signal by each operator's wall-clock
+runtime instead; because that runtime is not seed-controlled, runs with the
+same seed could differ, a defect since corrected
+(Sect.~\ref{sec:integrity}).
+
+Two sets are involved. The initial solution is built once per day by a
+single profit-aware greedy construction with a per-run seed, and the colony
+searches transitions over eleven modification operators (relocate, swap,
+2-opt, Or-opt chains, swap*, string and Shaw removals, perturbation, and
+their profit-aware variants); the saved keys that would restrict the
+operator set or shorten journeys were not honoured, so every run uses all
+eleven operators and journeys of length eleven. The search objective that
+ranks solutions is the routing cost plus a capacity penalty that is halved
+each time the search stagnates and restored on improvement --- there is no
+revenue term in it, so which bins are worth collecting is decided by the
+profit-aware operators, not by the objective; the oscillating penalty lets
+the search revisit inclusion decisions it would otherwise be trapped away
+from. In the archived implementation the penalty could relax the capacity
+bound altogether once halved below its initial value, and the returned plan
+was the penalised-objective incumbent without a final capacity re-check, so
+an over-capacity route could be returned and executed; this is corrected
+since, and flagged in Sect.~\ref{sec:integrity}. The saved configurations
+use ten ants, $\alpha = 1$, $\beta = 2$, initial pheromone $\phi_0 = 1$,
+$r_{\mathrm{evap}} = 0.5$, deposit constant $Q_{\mathrm{dep}} = 1.0$ (the
+saved $0.9$ never reached the solver), elitism ratio $0.5$, a stagnation
+limit of ten, at most $50$ iterations, and a $60$\,s budget; which of these
+the archived executable effectively applied is pending recovery of the run
+records.
 
